## 6. Where I disagree with the reviewer (not applied)
1. b#2 — ACCEPTED-nl's shorthand "nabestaanden → 'A en B' (the parties themselves)" is
   factually wrong: B died at the scene, A was unconscious in hospital, and the English source
   says "The families of A and B". Written as "De familie van A en de nabestaanden van B".
2. a#16 / a#50 / a#51 — the reviewer wants "Nederland of België" named inside the columns as the
   reader's country. Besides tripping checkNationality, naming the reader's jurisdiction in a
   Taiwan-law column invites a reader to assume something is being said about Dutch/Belgian law.
   Neutral R2 phrasing used instead.
3. a#23 — "consultatie" is not only EU/medical Dutch; "consultatietaal" is a defined, glossed
   term of this product used in all 27 locales, and replacing it piecemeal would make the page
   inconsistent with its own definition list. Only the CTA/heading was changed.
4. b#4/b#6 suggest spelling out "tien"/"twee" for 10/2. Done for 015's "tien
   bedrijfsactiviteiten" (the checker parses Dutch number words), but digits were kept where the
   sentence is a legal threshold list (e.g. "1 of 2 bestuurders", "2 aandeelhouders of meer"),
   because that is how Dutch statutory prose writes them and it keeps number parity obvious.
5. a#40 — the reviewer would translate the degree lines fully. Institution names (Institute of
   Finance, National Taiwan University, Kobe University) are kept in Latin script per R5; only
   the Dutch framing of the degree was added.
